# XCU 1.4 Utility Description Defaults
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The defaults of XCU 1.4 apply to every standard utility; the tests probe a
# representative set of mandatory utilities (each named utility is BASE).

assertion  XCU.1.4-utility-defaults.001
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4/unrecognized by the implementation
class      required
since      1992
says       A standard utility given an option it does not recognize, or an option
           that needs an argument without one, writes a diagnostic to standard
           error and exits non-zero.
note       A utility that accepts the probe option as an extension is skipped; at
           least three of the probed utilities must reject it.
test       t/001-option-diagnostics.sh

assertion  XCU.1.4-utility-defaults.002
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4/eight-bit transparency
class      required
since      1992
says       Utilities process command-line arguments containing arbitrary non-NUL
           bytes (including 0x01, 0x7f, 0x80 and 0xff) without altering them.
test       t/002-args-eight-bit.sh

assertion  XCU.1.4-utility-defaults.003
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4/eight-bit transparency
class      required
since      1992
says       Utilities process input files containing every byte value except NUL
           and newline without altering them.
test       t/003-input-eight-bit.sh

assertion  XCU.1.4-utility-defaults.004
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4/eight-bit transparency
class      required
since      1992
says       Utilities process environment variable names and values containing
           bytes with the high bit set without altering them.
test       t/004-env-eight-bit.sh

assertion  XCU.1.4-utility-defaults.005
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4/first argument to be discarded
class      required
since      1992
says       Standard utilities that accept no options but accept operands treat a
           first argument of two hyphens as the end of options and discard it, so
           operands may start with a hyphen.
test       t/005-double-hyphen.sh

assertion  XCU.1.4-utility-defaults.006
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4/process those operands
class      required
since      1992
says       Standard utilities process their operands in the order given on the
           command line: cat, wc, head, tail, cksum and grep report files in
           operand order.
test       t/006-operand-order.sh

assertion  XCU.1.4-utility-defaults.007
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4/Not used
class      required
since      1992
says       Utilities whose STDIN section reads "Not used" do not read standard
           input when used as described: data waiting in a pipe is still there
           afterwards.
test       t/007-stdin-not-used.sh

assertion  XCU.1.4-utility-defaults.008
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4/Not used
class      required
since      1992
says       Utilities whose STDOUT section reads "Not used" write nothing to
           standard output when used as described.
test       t/008-stdout-not-used.sh

assertion  XCU.1.4-utility-defaults.009
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4/diagnostic messages shall be sent
class      required
since      1992
says       When a standard utility is used as described and no error occurs, it
           writes nothing to standard error.
test       t/009-stderr-diagnostics-only.sh

assertion  XCU.1.4-utility-defaults.010
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4/shall not depend on the existence
class      required
since      1992
says       The output and exit status of filters such as cat, sort and wc do not
           depend on unrelated environment variables defined by the standard,
           such as ENV, FCEDIT, HISTFILE, HISTSIZE, MAIL, MAILCHECK, MAILPATH,
           PS1, PS2, PS4, COLUMNS, LINES, CDPATH, OPTIND and OPTARG.
test       t/010-env-independence.sh

assertion  XCU.1.4-utility-defaults.011
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4/properly positioned
class      required
since      2024
says       When a standard utility reads a seekable input file and ends without
           error before end-of-file, the file offset is left just past the last
           byte it processed, so (sed -n 1q; cat) gives what tail -n +2 gives;
           sed 2q, head -n 2 and dd bs=3 count=1 behave likewise.
test       t/011-file-offset.sh

assertion  XCU.1.4-utility-defaults.012
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4/properly positioned
class      required
since      2024
says       The read utility of the shell leaves a seekable standard input
           positioned just past the line it consumed, and a shell reading
           commands from such an input leaves the rest for the commands it runs.
test       t/012-file-offset-read.sh

assertion  XCU.1.4-utility-defaults.013
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4/properly positioned
class      required
since      2024
says       awk leaves a seekable standard input positioned just past the last
           record it processed when it exits before end-of-file.
test       t/013-file-offset-awk.sh

assertion  XCU.1.4-utility-defaults.014
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4/standard action
class      required
since      1992
says       A utility whose ASYNCHRONOUS EVENTS section says Default and that
           starts with SIGTERM or SIGHUP ignored keeps ignoring it: sleep, and
           cat and sort blocked on a FIFO, survive the signal.
timeout    60
test       t/014-signal-ignored-inherited.sh

assertion  XCU.1.4-utility-defaults.015
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4/standard action
class      required
since      1992
says       A utility whose ASYNCHRONOUS EVENTS section says Default and that
           starts with SIGTERM or SIGHUP at the default action is terminated by
           that signal: sleep, and cat and sort blocked on a FIFO, report a
           signal-termination status.
timeout    60
test       t/015-signal-default-inherited.sh

assertion  XCU.1.4-utility-defaults.016
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4/removed by the implementation
class      required
since      1992
says       Temporary files that standard utilities create in TMPDIR are removed
           on both successful exit and exit because of errors: after sort, ed,
           sed, awk and diff finish, nothing is left in the directory.
timeout    60
test       t/016-temp-files-removed.sh

assertion  XCU.1.4-utility-defaults.017
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4/temporary files
class      required
since      1992
says       Several instances of the same utility running at once in one working
           directory and sharing one TMPDIR do not disturb each other: six
           concurrent sorts each produce the correct output.
timeout    120
test       t/017-temp-files-concurrent.sh

assertion  XCU.1.4-utility-defaults.018
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4/continue processing the next operand
class      required
since      1992
says       When the requested action cannot be performed on one operand, the
           utility writes a diagnostic, goes on to the next operand and exits
           with a non-zero status: cat, rm, chmod, wc, head, cksum, mv, rmdir,
           ls, cp, mkdir and touch.
test       t/018-operand-failure-continues.sh

assertion  XCU.1.4-utility-defaults.019
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4/cannot be performed
class      required
since      1992
says       When the action requested by an option or option-argument cannot be
           performed, the utility writes a diagnostic and exits non-zero: sort -o
           into a missing directory, and sed, grep and awk given an unreadable
           script or pattern file.
test       t/019-option-action-fails.sh

assertion  XCU.1.4-utility-defaults.020
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4/unrecoverable
class      required
since      1992
says       When a utility meets an error (a missing operand file or an impossible
           target), it writes a diagnostic to standard error and exits with a
           non-zero status.
test       t/020-error-diagnostic-status.sh

assertion  XCU.1.4-utility-defaults.021
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4/declaration utility
class      required
since      2024
says       A standard utility other than those the standard names as declaration
           utilities is not treated as one: with printf, echo and env, a word
           such as name=~/x gets no tilde expansion and a word such as name=$var
           is split into fields.
test       t/021-not-declaration-utility.sh
